The Journal of Coren Vael


The Marsh Remembers
I don’t remember the day I was born, but the marsh does.
They say my father came home wild-eyed, lost in the fog, and my mother wept when she saw me — one eye blue, the other green.
The mark of something wrong.
The midwives whispered the old words: changeling.
I never knew the hag’s name then, only that the reeds would move when there was no wind.
Sometimes, at night, I’d wake to the sound of something whispering my name in the water.
Edrin. Soft. Patient.
The marsh never forgot me, even when my parents tried to.
When I was twelve, I vanished for a few days. When I came back, there was a mark on my chest, just under my ribs— like the twisted roots of a tree.
They sent me away soon after.
My father pressed a silver dagger into my hand.
If you hear her again, you cut the sound away. Don’t listen. Don’t follow.” he said.
He didn’t look me in the eye.
I think he knew he wasn’t giving me a weapon. He was giving me distance.
________________


The Caravan
They were desert scavengers — the kind who traded in broken tools and broken people.
They took me in because I was small, fast, and quiet.
I learned fast, too — it was the only way to stay fed.
Voss Dren, the caravan master, said mercy was for those who could afford it.
He didn’t want me killing at first — that was wasteful.
He wanted proof. Trophies.
Each time we came across travellers, he’d send me into the dark to take from them.
Coin, jewels, water — whatever they carried.
But I had to bring something back that proved I’d done the work myself.
A ring finger, a toe, an ear — something that made the others flinch when they saw it.
If I brought nothing, I didn’t eat.
If I came back empty-handed twice, I didn’t drink.
You learn a lot about people when you take from them piece by piece.
Mostly, that no one wants to die, but everyone wants to be remembered.
________________


The Wanderer
One night, the desert brought me a stranger.
He wore scavenger’s leathers and carried a lantern of old-world glass that glowed faintly blue.
He said his name was Coren Vael, a relic hunter searching for machines buried in the shadows of the Coal Iron Columns — gods-old engines that still whispered beneath the dust.
Voss sent me to watch him, to see what he carried that might be worth taking.
But I saw something else.
A man with direction. A name that sounded like it belonged somewhere.
I hadn’t felt that in years.
When he made camp beneath a rusted overpass, I waited until his fire burned low.
I crept close, thin from hunger, shaking with exhaustion and fear.
The strike wasn’t clean — a struggle, a flash of steel, a muffled cry — and then the desert took him.
I searched his satchel and found a few small things: a half-empty flask, a rusted compass, and a folded parchment sealed in wax.
A train ticket.
The name on it read Coren Vael.
I said it aloud under the stars, just to hear what it felt like in my mouth.
When I did, something changed.
The air went still.
The whispers that had followed me since Dreadmere — her voice, that low, drowning murmur in my blood — went quiet.
For the first time in my life, there was silence.
As if the marsh itself had forgotten me.
That night, I buried the real Coren in the sand.
Then I scrubbed the mud from my skin, packed what little he’d carried, and walked toward the iron tracks gleaming faintly in the distance.








________________


The Train
I don’t remember boarding. Just the sound — the hiss, the hum, the wind howling past the desert glass.
No one looked at me.
No one questioned the ticket.
It was as if the world agreed I could be someone else.
I told myself I’d make the name mean something.
Coren Vael. Not the hag’s son. Not a thief.
Just a man going somewhere better.
When the train reached Abram’s Rest, I saw the city for what it was: a machine built on thirst.
Two levels — one drowning in luxury, one in dust.
The Baron owned the water, and the people below drank debt.
I didn’t belong anywhere, but at least no one called me Edrin.
________________


The Mines
They put me to work below the streets, digging coal and iron.
The air was heavy and hot — every breath tasted like ash.
The overseers carried whips and smiles in equal measure.
I tried to stay invisible, but some things don’t fade easy.
My eyes. My quiet. My hunger.
The miners called me “ghost.”
I started protecting the weak ones — quietly, out of sight.
Made some guards trip, lose their coin, disappear into dark tunnels.
They never saw the knife.
They never saw me.
Rumors started — a spirit in the shafts, taking the cruel.
It wasn’t long before the rumors found ears sharper than the Baron’s.
________________






The Silent Knives
They came one night — masked, quiet, precise.
I thought they’d come to kill me.
Instead, they offered me a hand.
Tallis led them — calm voice, soldier’s eyes.
He said, “If you’re going to haunt the dark, learn to use it.”
The Knives didn’t steal for greed. They stole for balance.
Water for the thirsty. Food for the hungry. Vengeance for the desperate.
They taught me to listen, to think before I moved.
For the first time, I belonged somewhere.
For the first time, the shadows felt like home.
________________


The Lesson in the Dust
The job was meant to be simple — a test.
Six barrels of water, hoarded by a merchant named Derris Vann, meant for the miners.
Recover them. No noise. No blood.
I failed both.
A hinge screamed, a lantern fell, and the barrels burst.
Water hissed into the dust until there was nothing left but steam and guilt.
When Tallis found me, he didn’t yell.
He just said, “Patience saves more lives than speed.”
Then he walked away.
Ressa stayed long enough to add, “A thief’s worth is in what he doesn’t destroy.”
I carry that lesson like a scar no one sees.
________________


Ash and Water
The city still smells of smoke.
I can’t forget the sound those barrels made — like thunder with nothing to rain on.
We meant to give people hope.
Instead, I gave them nothing but a reminder of how fragile it is.
Tallis was right.
The Knives don’t move fast. They move right.
 I’m still learning how.
Every failure feels heavier than the last, but I keep walking.
Maybe redemption isn’t a place you reach.
Maybe it’s the road you walk with empty hands.
________________


The Upper City
That’s where I met Jareth Elenor.
A battlemage with a soldier’s poise and a scholar’s curiosity.
Tallis paired us on the jobs that needed both steel and silence.
At first, it was all work — maps, routes, rotations.
Then, late nights in his quarters, watching him draw runes by candlelight.
He said I was too quiet. I told him he talked enough for both of us.
Somewhere in that silence, we stopped pretending we weren’t reaching for the same kind of peace.
It wasn’t sudden. It was slow. Steady. Real.
For the first time, I stopped feeling like a ghost.
________________


Knives in the Smoke
Months passed.
We became the Knives’ best pair — he above, me below.
Jareth’s magic, my blades. Clean work. No mistakes.
He started talking about freedom — not just from the Baron, but from her.
 He’d seen the way I woke some nights, breath caught in my throat, the brand burning cold.
He said he could find a way to break it.
I told him not to.
He didn’t listen.
Love makes fools of us all.
And the hag loves fools most of all.
________________


The Night the Shadows Spoke
The job was bait — the Baron’s men waiting with runes and rifles.
Jareth refused to come. Said he had found something more important.
A ritual. A way to “free” me.
I went alone.
The ambush came fast — torches, shouting, blood. I ran until the city blurred.
When I reached the safehouse, the air was green.
He was in the circle, chanting, shaking, eyes bright with madness and love.
He called my name — Coren.
 And that’s all she needed.
She came through him like a whisper turned inside out.
Her voice, his mouth. Her laughter, his tears.
You took his name, my sweet. I only came to claim it back.
The rest is a blur of smoke, steel, and screaming.
When the light died, he was on the floor — bleeding, but breathing.
I grabbed his ring and ran.
________________


The Marsh Dream
I still see him sometimes.
Half-submerged in dark water, eyes green as the marsh.
His mouth moves, but it’s her voice that comes out.
“You left him,” it says.
“You left me.”
The mist curls around my feet, cold and familiar.
Then I wake.
Always before she reaches me.
________________




The Rail-Song
Another dream, he was there again—half-submerged, eyes too green, mouth moving with her voice. You left him. You left me. I sat up before she could reach me, but the room still felt wet, as if the air itself remembered the marsh.
The ring was in my fist when I came to. I don’t remember taking it out in the night, only the ache in my palm where the metal bit too hard. I rubbed it clean anyway, like polish could turn it into an answer.
Downstairs, over thin beer and thicker lies, I heard the rumor drift in on the rails—quiet transfer, prisoner taken alive, a mage under guard. No names. Just the shape of one. Men swallow details when they’re afraid of what repeating them might summon.
I told myself it was nothing. Station talk. Dust-born hope.
Then I looked at the ring again and realized hope isn’t something you carry. It’s something that drags.
So I bought the ticket.
Abram’s Rest is a throat that swallows people whole—yet my feet are already walking back toward it.
— Coren
