Desert Rose (First Session)
I boarded the train for Abram’s Rest and kept to myself, hood up, eyes down. The ring felt like a stone in my pocket — a reminder that hope can get you killed if you wear it too openly.
My carriage was crowded with trouble: a minotaur in shackles, a man with an ape, a younger elf and an older one who looks like she’d bite before she’d smile, and a fellow with a robot clicking along beside him. The older elf passed bread to the younger one like it meant something. Another quiet ritual I don’t understand.
Then the windows exploded.
Six goblins came through the glass. I dropped one with a clean Twin Feint before the rest tore the others down. After the fight, the younger elf started rubbing mud onto the older one’s wounds. It actually helped — which only made it stranger. The older elf didn’t look thankful. She just looked… inconvenienced.
An engineer found us shaking in the wreck and said every other passenger was dead. The goblins stole the magicite core. Without it, the train’s as good as a coffin on rails. Options were thrown around: Pem, thirty miles west, or a temple northeast where the prisoner claimed there might be raw magicite (and “treasure,” of course).
We’re likely going after the goblins.
I don’t care which way this turns, not really. I just need to reach Abram’s Rest — quickly — before the past decides it’s done waiting for me.
— Coren
________________
Rabbit Rock Road (Second Session)
The raccoon turned into a dog. Verdant Shade scooped it up like luggage, and it tried to sniff out a trail. All it found was shit. The monkey proudly declared he’s an “experienced tracker.” I’m learning that absurdity travels well in the desert.
We headed east — ten miles or so before the sun began to die. The monkey found half-eaten rock rabbits, left gnawed and wasted. Rabbit Rock Mountains, then. Goblins nesting in stone.
We camped and finally traded names like that makes strangers safer:
Chango K Caza. Hylanku Lonehorn. Lira Cervan. Luxem. Quercucoonie.
I told them I’m chasing a rumour. Close enough to truth to pass.
I took the first watch. The sands don’t forgive sleep. Luxem still carries herself like all of this is beneath her — inconvenienced by danger rather than threatened by it. The prisoner pushed Chango about delivering him to the Baron. I warned them: you don’t want the Baron deciding you’re his problem. I don’t think my tone betrayed how personal that is. The prisoner also cautioned against showing magic too openly. He hinted he knows things that could challenge the Baron… but “not yet.”
Quercucoonie said he’s going to Abram’s Rest to reforest his home and learn why it’s dying. Hylanku answered in a tongue I couldn’t follow. Lira is searching for something she’s lost. Luxem said nothing. The prisoner called her thorny. Accurate.
My watch stayed quiet. Lira came to take over, finally I slept. No dreams. Thank the knives.
The following morning we found a woman alone by a fire, drowning herself in drink — Jane, leather jacket and hat, eyes like burnt paper. Lira reached her. Jane’s family was butchered by the Baron’s men hunting mages — husband and son cut down in the sand.
If they have Jareth…
We reached the goblin camp. Hylanku thinks they’re deeper in, but they were all dead. Luxem caused a statue to come alive and it dropped her cold. I threw myself in over her body, blade flashing, expecting stone to crack like bone.
It didn’t.
— Coren
________________


Fresh Water and Cold Stone (Third Session)
Nathan Indiana Croft asked to be let loose again. He didn’t beg—he negotiated, like the shackles were just another inconvenience. He warned us how to pull the magicite from the mechanical creature without it taking our hands with it. Chango finally relented.
We took turns prying at the thing’s chest cavity. I tried more times than I care to admit. Missed. Again. Even my blades couldn’t find purchase—steel sliding off angles like the creature was built to humiliate you. Frustrating in a way that crawls under the skin.
Quercucoonie got it in the end. Simple, steady, no drama. The magicite came free like a tooth pulled from a stubborn jaw.
We searched the camp after. Nothing worth the risk, but we refilled our waterskins. Fresh water. Rarer than diamonds, some would say, and they’d be right out here.
Now we’re heading back to the train. Hylanku is carrying Luxem—she’s slow to stir after the statue dropped her. Part of me wants to splash water in her face just to be sure she’s alive. The other part doesn’t want to see what “thorny” looks like when it wakes angry and wet.
Maybe she’s sleeping properly. Maybe her dreams are kinder than mine.
— Coren
________________


Ayudara (Fourth Session)
Nathan has a word for everything the rest of us just call wrong. The metal thing we fought back at the camp wasn’t just a machine — it was ancient. Setantii. He studies the old world the way some people study saints, and he spoke about it like it isn’t truly dead… just buried.
He also spoke about the Baron — how obsessed he is with the Drop beneath Abram’s Rest. All that lost technology, all that power, and the Baron’s certainty that he can make it work for him. It’s hard not to notice how every thread out here seems tied to that same fist. Everyone I meet has a scar that somehow leads back to him.
Not long after, we found an archway cut into a tight crevice, stone worked by hands that didn’t belong to the desert. An elf gate. Ayudara. They said the elves used them to leave this world — a road back to their home planet. A door out. I tried not to stare too long. Doors like that make you think dangerous thoughts.
The ground answered first.
Bones pushed up through the sand as if the earth was spitting them out: four skeletons and a captain rising in a dry chorus of grit and snapping joints. We formed up fast. The others are good in a fight — better than I expected from a train-carriage of strangers. Steel rang, spells cracked, the dead kept coming until they didn’t.
I got one clean opening on the captain. One swing. I cut through its neck and the thing folded, collapsing back into the sand like it had never been anything but a pile of bad history.
Then, when the last skull stopped rolling… silence.
And in that silence, she spoke again. Not to the others. To me. She’s found me again.
— Coren
________________


Back Into Abram’s Rest (Fifth Session Part 1)
We all woke with sand in our joints and yesterday still clinging to us. I didn’t hear her voice again. The silence was almost worse — like the desert was holding its breath.
Lira sat with dirt and sprigs between her fingers, coaxing life out of dead ground the way druids do. Quercucoonie entertained himself with rocks like they were telling secrets. Chango took Nathan aside for a quiet talk I wasn’t invited to, then cleaned and reloaded his guns with the kind of care you only give something you trust.
We made it back to the Desert Rose. Thistog had been busy — the dead passengers were removed and buried properly. Respect, in a place that doesn’t offer much of it. He looked genuinely surprised we returned at all. We handed over the magicite. The train shuddered, groaned, and finally remembered how to live.
Abram’s Rest by the end of the day.
Then I can see if the rumours are true.
We took a first-class carriage this time. Felt wrong, sitting in velvet while the world outside boils. Chango found a pocket watch — initials T.R. — but none of us knew the name. The kind of small mystery that sticks in your mind because everything else is too sharp.
Nathan warned the others again: don’t make it obvious you can use magic. The Baron enslaves magic users. I kept my face still when he said it. Just another reason to get back quickly. 
The ravine appeared first, then watchtowers on the horizon like teeth. We hadn’t even left the train and my body started doing what it remembers: shrinking, hiding, becoming smaller than the attention around me. At the gate, refugees begged to be let in. Guards aimed guns at them as if hunger was contagious.
We were funnelled into the Hexes and met our escort — Phil Philliamson — leaning against a wall like he’d been waiting his whole life for this exact moment. Wheat stalk in his mouth, lazy posture, eyes that didn’t miss anything. “Follow me,” he said. 
He told us if we wanted work, check the Dancing Cat.
The city feels tighter than I remember. More militia. More eyes. Stalls selling cooked risen — lizard-meat on sticks — and Quercucoonie bought a live one for reasons I won’t pretend to understand. Caged animals too, packed in like they’re already dead. Lira didn’t like that. I don’t blame her.
At the Dancing Cat, the tengu barman—Pok—hadn’t heard anything about a captured mage. Which worries me. Rumours travel faster than trains in this city. If tavern staff don’t know it, either it’s false… or it’s being smothered. Hylanku and Lira got Luxem a room and promised to call a doctor from the Cliffs. She’s been out since the statue dropped her. I hope her dreams are gentle. I don’t expect mine to be.
Phil—“Crosswind Phil,” apparently—showed his scars like badges and implied he’s the kind of shot who doesn’t miss. One shot, one kill. He said it like a prayer.
Then Ressa walked in.
I approached before I could stop myself.
She was angry — thought I’d abandoned the Silent Knives. Said they’ve been wiped out since the coup. Wiped out. She was spared, though. The Baron gave her a foreman job like that makes mercy. I told her about my last job — how it was a trap, how I ended it with a blade in Jareth. She hasn’t heard Jareth was dead.
That landed wrong in my gut.
If the city doesn’t know, then maybe someone has kept him quiet. Maybe the Professor has him — the one who digs in the Drop and pulls nightmares into daylight. Experiments. Technology. Potentially people. Jareth is a mage. If he’s alive, he’s useful. If he’s dead, he’s a lesson.
Ressa offered to help us find work. Quercucoonie asked about jobs “related to the mind.” She said she could get him survey work. Lira asked her to help contact Luddy, pitmaster of the coliseum.
I asked Pok about the Professor. He called him the brains to the Baron’s brawn. Said the mining has worsened since he arrived — deeper, closer to the Darklands. I asked where the library is. “The Cobbles,” he said.
The Cobbles are where information lives. And information is how you cut a city open without anyone seeing the knife.
Next day, Quercucoonie went to the blacksmith — a Venaran, with features that made him look like he could be Chango’s blood if you squint. Quercucoonie bought a crossbow. Chango asked why a Venaran would be here. Debt. The Baron again. Chango talked like he wanted to free him. The Venaran didn’t seem interested in hope.
When Chango stepped outside, a raven landed on him like it owned him.
Phil finally admitted what his posture’s been whispering since we met: he wants the Baron gone. Wants Abram’s Rest freed from his reign of terror. But he still won’t tell us who his benefactor is — or why they’ve put him on us like a leash.
I needed answers, not speeches.
So I went to scout the Cobbles.
Blended into shadow like old muscle memory. It’s been too long. The adrenaline hit clean and bright — almost joyful, which is its own kind of sickness. I watched the guards, counted them, mapped their rotation, learned the rhythm of the wall like it was a heartbeat.
Then I went to Ressa and tried to secure passes the polite way. She said she’ll try.
I offered her help in return — for any problems she needs solved.
That’s the thing about Abram’s Rest: nothing is free. Not water. Not safety.
Not even favours.
— Coren
________________


 Abram’s Unrest (Fifth Session Part 2)
Hylanku and Lira asked if we’d join them in a fight at the coliseum. Work, coin, cover — all the same thing in this city. We said yes.
Quercucoonie asked the question that keeps getting people killed: should magic be used in the arena? At the word magic Phil’s face flickered blue and the air around him made a sound like a wire pulled tight. He admitted he was imbued when he was injured — magic stitched him back together. Saved his life. Left a mark that still answers when the subject comes up.
Lira’s reason was simpler. Her elk is imprisoned there. Thallion. She wants him back.
Quercucoonie went shopping for disguise — a cloak and hair dye so he can hide what he is if he has to cast. The dye job came out… orange. Loud, bright, unforgivable. If the Baron doesn’t notice magic, he’ll notice that.
I still haven’t seen a wanted poster with my face on it. Either they never knew who they were hunting… or they think I’m dead. I don’t know which is safer.
The coliseum stank of old blood and fresh fear. They gave us a tour like cruelty is a feature: people and animals in cages, waiting their turn to be entertainment. Thallion was there. Calm in a way that hurt to see — like he’d decided this was simply how the world is now. Lira talked, half-mad with anger, about trading Hylanku for the elk. Thallion was earmarked for Professor Godt.
So we have the Professor’s name now.
We registered under a name that fit the city better than its own: Abram’s Unrest.
Five opponents. Returning champions.
The fight was ugly. Hylanku went down. I dropped an orc — clean — but he got back up like the dead do. Phil put three of them in the dirt for good, including the orc I thought I’d finished. Quercucoonie worked the crowd like a jester with a knife hidden in his sleeve, buying Lira time to drag Hylanku back from the edge.
We won.
As agreed, they released Thallion into the arena. He went straight to Lira the moment his hooves hit sand. Reunited — finally — for the space of a heartbeat.
Then the sky tore open.
Screeches overhead. Two giant bats dropped into the arena and others started tearing people apart like the crowd was meat laid out for them. Panic rolled through the coliseum in a single wave.
Chango went down. Quercucoonie went down. Hylanku hefted Quercucoonie’s unconscious body like it weighed nothing. Thallion ran to Chango, lowered his head, and somehow got him up — scooped and hauled him like a comrade.
And above it all, on a massive white bat, a red-skinned humanoid watched the carnage like it was theatre and shouted: “For too long you have invaded our domain!”
Not bandits. Not goblins. Something else. Something older. Something angry.
I ran to the gate and held there, waiting, making myself the last knife in the doorway. Lira went down. Hylanku took her and ran. Thallion followed — and somehow, without words, told me what needed doing.
I closed the gate behind them.
Phil was at the opposite side. I shouted for him to do the same. He did — then he stopped, raised his gun, and shot at a bat instead of running. Stubborn as stone. He finally brought one down and only then moved.
We ran for the Dancing Cat with the city screaming behind us.
Abram’s Rest calls itself a fortress.
Tonight it felt like a cage that just learned it has claws.
— Coren
________________


Warning on White Wings (Session 6)
Hylanku threw a spell up into the night and the bats’ attention fractured for a heartbeat. I used that heartbeat. Up the wall, onto the roof — loose tiles under my boots, blood-slick air in my lungs — ready to be the loudest thing in the street if it kept the others moving. Below, Thallion did what no creature should have to learn: he seized a barrel like a weapon. Hylanku and the elk slipped past in the chaos, and I followed the line they’d cut through fear.
Pok and Ressa opened the Dancing Cat just wide enough to swallow us. Before I dropped down, I looked back.
The red rider had settled on a tower in the city’s heart, horns on his armour catching the firelight, trident held like judgement. He wasn’t hunting prey. He was making a point.
Inside, Yangu the blacksmith was there, and Luxem still hadn’t woken. A figure in a long coat moved among the injured — mask like a beak, goggles that reflected candlelight. Plague-doctor. He asked if we were hurt, then healed without a collar, warmth rolling out from him like breath. Chango came back to himself with a curse and a laugh that sounded a little broken. He said he needed a break. None of us argued.
Thallion found Lira and pressed his forehead to hers. She held him like the world couldn’t steal him again. Ressa gave us vague work permits and one gate pass, the kind that means everything and nothing. She warned Lira to hide the elk. Lira said no.
I asked Ressa what happened to the Silent Knives. She took me into her office with a bottle and a hard stare. I told her more about that last job — the trap, Jareth not coming, fixated on a spell. I asked her what she knew about the marsh. She said only fairytales. I told her fairytales aren’t always lies—then left it there. She said if she learns I had anything to do with the massacre, she’ll kill me. I told her I’d hand her the blade myself.
Later, Quercucoonie fed his pet a seed, and the little thing eased, just a fraction. When he realised Verdant Shade was gone, roots crawled up the floorboards and shaped themselves into the eidolon again — snapping free like a plant deciding to walk.
Lira tried a fortune—what will the bats do tomorrow?—and it failed. Then the answer came anyway, from outside, in the rider’s voice: “Take this as warning. Do not enter Nar-Voth again.” Lira knew the name. Darklands. Nar-Voth. The bats began to retreat.
We stepped back out into the Hexes and found the streets littered with bodies, stalls smashed, shopkeepers fighting off looters.
No guard bodies.
Abram’s Rest is still smiling like it’s in control. But I’ve seen the teeth now.
— Coren


The Riad (Session 7)
We went back to the station and counted guards. Quercucoonie cast something to check for hidden ones. A guard noticed and shot the ground in front of him.
A short, chubby bastard with a handlebar moustache gave us ten seconds to explain ourselves. Quercucoonie played scared well enough — bats, work permits, Ressa, the Dancing Cat. It worked. Mostly.
They assigned us Gasskin, tall and gaunt, to watch us. The gate opened with giant metal cogs, and the Cobbles showed their teeth in a prettier way than the Hexes. Clean stone. Trees. Bushes. Two fountains wasting water like decoration.
We were sent to move bodies from the garden.
Hylanku searched for Foxfire, the red-haired slave-mage. Instead, we found a black-haired woman in a rusted runic collar, her magic forced through her fingers to mend a damaged tree. Hylanku asked where they kept the mages. She pointed to the dam, then northwest near the houses before the library. He asked her name.
“They took it,” she said.
That stayed with me.
Quercucoonie learned Professor Godt hasn’t been seen much, and no one gets near the library without permission. I remember an old civil library far west from my time here. Seems clear enough now.
Godt’s library.
While shifting the dead, I found a pass on a nobleman and pocketed it. Waste not.
Gasskin offered more work: warehouses or the Riad, a bathhouse for the Baron’s loyal pets. Its defence protocol had triggered during the bat attack, and something was still inside. At the entrance stood Celine Von Basten — silk, jewellery, rifle, and all the charm of a knife in the ribs. The Baron’s daughter. She threatened the guards, looked Hylanku over like meat, then called Quercucoonie starters.
So the rot has heirs.
Quercucoonie prayed to Orth before we entered. Asked to be saved from the tyranny of man and beast. In Abram’s Rest, that doesn’t narrow it down.
I picked the lock. Clean. Quiet. Satisfying.
Inside, we found Five clay gargoyles wandering the bathhouse like old violence given shape. Hylanku shielded us, and we took them apart piece by piece — steel, spell, grit, and Verdant Shade tearing an aquamarine eye from the last one’s skull.
Pretty thing, pulled from an ugly place.
That’s Abram’s Rest all over.
* Coren 






The Safehouse (Session 8)
The Riad had more secrets under its polished stone.
Quercucoonie found nothing through the roots, but the bodies told enough. Two dead priests in a boiling pool. One torn open, the other untouched except for ripped robes and gloves too clean for death. I pulled him out and took them. White cloth, past the wrist. Magical, maybe. Or just expensive enough to get someone killed.
The floor nearly took Quercucoonie next. Pipes below. Tunnels. Tracks for the homunculi. He slipped down in that raccoon-dog shape and found empty armour standing guard in the dark.
We searched deeper. More bodies. Scratches. A potion of water breathing. Then the bat finally came, dropping on Hylanku near a pool where a couple and child were hiding. We cut, clawed, shot, and gored it until Hylanku’s horns tore through its chin and sent it into the water.
We took the survivors’ passes as reward. Four now.
Outside, we threw the bat’s head at the guards’ feet. Gasskin seemed pleased enough. Train, coliseum, bathhouse — apparently we’re useful now. Useful enough to be given a flat in the Cobbles.
Third floor.
I knew the place before I wanted to.
Jareth’s safehouse.
Different furniture. Same bones. Same entry hall where the spell circle burned into the wood. Same room where I stabbed him and ran. In his old chest, hidden where only I’d know to look, I found a charcoal drawing.
Me.
I tried scouting the library that night. Made noise, drew guards, took a shot, ran back bleeding.
Tomorrow’s plan is better.
Abram’s Rest keeps opening old doors.
None of them lead forward.
