The Library Window (Session 9)
Morning came quietly, which felt like a trick.
I woke in Jareth’s old safehouse with the spell-scar still under the floorboards and his drawing hidden away. Abram’s Rest had started pretending to be normal again. Stalls opening. Voices returning. People stepping around bloodstains like they were only rain.
I went to the Galleries looking for old memories.
A cobbler I recognised remembered the day the Silent Knives fell. He saw guards carrying Jareth’s body from the safehouse — then a man with long silver hair and a scarred cheek took control. Lorcend Von Basten. The Baron’s son.
He had Jareth taken to the library.
Not buried. Not burned.
Taken.
The cobbler spoke of experiments there. Slaves and mages going in, never coming back out. I asked if anyone taken there was ever seen again.
No.
Hope is a cruel thing. It hooks under the ribs and pulls.
I scoped the library in daylight. Bronze doors. Ornate glass. A control panel by the entrance. More old-world mansion than civic building. Magicite in its bones, most likely. Shutters closed tight.
But one window was open.
I climbed, looked inside, saw shelves and books. No guards. No answers.
Not yet.
I broke the latch before leaving.
Doors have teeth in Abram’s Rest.
But windows bleed.
